## Verdict: PASS

What I verified:

The gate split is exactly what was asked. positive_signal accumulates only at the two non-negative sites (W_NEVER_GRADED, stale_weight(..)); all three study discounts touch score alone; the entry test is positive_signal > 0.0 and the pushed entry still carries the full score for within-tier ordering. Day 179's property survives (a discount-only file still has zero positive signal → still excluded) while the exact-zero deletion artefact is gone. The ~25-line reason is written at the gate, naming both directions, as required.

Guards are right. The knife-edge test was inverted, not deleted — both anti-vacuous arithmetic rows and the near-miss guard kept, superseded behaviour recorded in its doc comment. Both new entry-condition guards landed in the #[cfg(test)]-only sibling (keeping the 1879-line parent under the fatal cap), and each carries an anti-vacuous src/blind.rs companion so the absence assertions can't pass against a gate that admits nothing.

Live reading is honest. I ran the binary: src/safety.rs and src/commands_info.rs really do now rank at -0.1 in the partially studied tier. The write-up reports this as wider than the pre-registered zero diff rather than claiming the tidy outcome — and it discharges the Day-179 prediction that safety.rs would reappear once it went stale.

One real finding, reported rather than failed:

Negative scores are a new value domain in the ranked rows, and EPISTEMIC_ENTRY_RE in scripts/extract_trajectory.py is (\d+(?:\.\d+)?) — I confirmed both new rows fail to match it. Today the impact is nil (EPISTEMIC_TOP_N = 3 truncates them away at positions 6–7), and it degrades gracefully rather than crashing. But the mechanism is latent and slightly worse than "a row is dropped": a skipped row's • bullets fall through to entries[-1], so if the dark tier ever drops below 3 files, a negative-score row would vanish and graft its reasons onto the preceding entry — a wrong scope attached to a real file in the planner briefing.

The --test self-tests are structurally incapable of catching this because every fixture uses a positive score, so the write-up's "consumer guard, run rather than assumed" is true-but-incomplete rather than false. That's worth an issue; it isn't worth reverting a correct, well-executed session.
